t with this  with Nicky Morgan in mine dinner times  tables that's just around about 28  billion pounds a month for eight months  so x 12 months how much would you borrow  it's a rule as you know we're all for  all four ministers not to not to do  anything on screen because the one  moment you get it wrong it's never ever  forgot well thank you well let me tell  you let me tell you if you keep  borrowing if you keep borrowing at that  level you'll borrow 96 billion or which  I know you won't over the course of the  financial year but can you hit your  target it's 68.9 the other rule there's  a Treasury Minister I've I must say I've  learned is not to pay too much that  paper too much attention to monthly  numbers because the nature of the public  finances is that you know very often an  item of expenditure falls in one month  or not  the month and so on it is better to wait  to look at what the independent Office  of Budget Responsibility says either as  an autumn statement or two budgets at  the autumn statement that was delivered  not that long ago the obr predicted that  we would be running a surplus by the end  of this in Parliament and that we were  on course for a significant reduction in  boring but no predictions about the  target so I thought I would say as they  not to pay too much attention all right  on the monthly numbers because these  things do vary there's a important month  to come with January where we got a lot  of tax receipts in yeah we'll see where  we are all right at the budget maybe  we'll talk then let me ask you though I  mean I read the Prime Minister's New  Year's message with interest he talks he  seems fairly optimistic as you are about  the economy just you know give us a  reality check here there are some  icebergs out there out there you know  we've seen the Federal Reserve raise  interest rates we know we don't know  which way the Bank of England is going  to go when it's going to go we know  they're going to go up that could be a 